"{\"eval_set_result_id\":\"TravelPlannerAgent_Evaluation_test_1_1763640957.7053363\",\"eval_set_result_name\":\"TravelPlannerAgent_Evaluation_test_1_1763640957.7053363\",\"eval_set_id\":\"Evaluation_test_1\",\"eval_case_results\":[{\"eval_set_file\":\"Evaluation_test_1\",\"eval_set_id\":\"Evaluation_test_1\",\"eval_id\":\"Test2\",\"final_eval_status\":1,\"eval_metric_results\":null,\"overall_eval_metric_results\":[{\"metric_name\":\"tool_trajectory_avg_score\",\"threshold\":0.5,\"judge_model_options\":null,\"criterion\":null,\"score\":1.0,\"eval_status\":1,\"details\":{\"rubric_scores\":null}},{\"metric_name\":\"response_match_score\",\"threshold\":0.5,\"judge_model_options\":null,\"criterion\":null,\"score\":0.5769442912300055,\"eval_status\":1,\"details\":{\"rubric_scores\":null}}],\"eval_metric_result_per_invocation\":[{\"actual_invocation\":{\"invocation_id\":\"e-ff2ce17f-0f93-4bce-8ad9-806f42dc722d\",\"user_content\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"Plan a 7-day cultural deep-dive travel experience in Kyoto focusing on temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature.\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"user\"},\"final_response\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"ITINERARY: ## 7-Day Kyoto Cultural Deep-Dive Itinerary\\n\\nThis itinerary is designed for a traveler seeking a rich cultural immersion, focusing on Kyoto's temples, traditional arts (tea ceremony), and natural beauty. It assumes an optimal travel period of Autumn (October/November) for comfortable weather and scenery.\\n\\n### 1. Suggested Accommodation Areas\\n\\nFor a cultural deep-dive, staying centrally or near major cultural hubs is ideal for minimizing travel time.\\n\\n*   **Gion/Higashiyama:** Perfect for atmosphere, proximity to Kiyomizu-Dera, Sannenzaka, and easy access to traditional restaurants and tea houses. **(Recommended for Atmosphere)**\\n*   **Downtown Kyoto (Kawaramachi/Shijo Area):** Excellent for convenience, transportation hub (bus/subway), shopping, and modern dining options, while still being a short bus ride from major sites. **(Recommended for Convenience)**\\n*   **Near Kyoto Station:** Best if you plan multiple day trips (e.g., Nara), but the area is modern and less atmospheric for evening strolls.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2. Detailed Daily Itinerary\\n\\n| Day | Focus Area & Location | Recommended Activities | Time Estimate | Meal Recommendations & Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Day 1** | **Arrival & Southern Higashiyama** | Arrive, check into accommodation, settle in. Visit **Kiyomizu-Dera Temple** (aim for late afternoon light). Walk down **Sannenzaka & Ninenzaka** preserved streets. Evening: Discreet exploration of **Gion District** (Hanamikoji Street). | Travel/Check-in (3 hrs). Kiyomizu-Dera (1.5 hrs). Streets/Shopping (1.5 hrs). Gion Stroll (1 hr). | **Dinner:** Seek out a traditional *kappo* (counter dining) experience in Gion or Pontocho Alley. **Cultural Tip:** Wear comfortable slip-ons for temples. |\\n| **Day 2** | **The Golden North & Zen Contemplation** | Morning visit to **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)** (arrive shortly after opening). Travel south to **Ryoan-ji Temple** for quiet contemplation of the Zen rock garden. Afternoon: Visit **Nijo-jo Castle** (Nightingale floors). | Kinkaku-ji (1.5 hrs). Travel (0.5 hr). Ryoan-ji (1 hr). Nijo-jo Castle (2 hrs). | **Lunch:** Try *Yudofu* (simmered tofu) near Ryoan-ji, a local vegetarian specialty. **Transport:** Bus heavy day. Purchase a Kyoto Bus Pass. |\\n| **Day 3** | **Torii Gates & Traditional Arts** | **Fushimi Inari Taisha:** Very early start (before 8:00 AM) to hike through the *torii* gates with fewer crowds. Midday: Return to central Kyoto. Afternoon: **Pre-booked Traditional Tea Ceremony** experience. | Fushimi Inari Hike (3-4 hrs). Travel/Rest (1 hr). Tea Ceremony (1.5 hrs). | **Lunch:** Inari area street food (e.g., *Inari Sushi*). **Dinner:** Explore the vibrant food stalls and restaurants around Nishiki Market (though the market closes early). |\\n| **Day 4** | **Western Nature Immersion (Arashiyama)** | Morning travel to **Arashiyama**. Visit **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple** and its magnificent landscape garden (UNESCO). Walk through the serene **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove**. Optional: Boat ride on the Hozugawa River or Sagano Scenic Railway (seasonal). | Travel (1 hr round trip). Tenry\u016b-ji (2 hrs). Bamboo Grove (1 hr). Optional Activity (2 hrs). | **Lunch:** Enjoy *Shojin Ryori* (Buddhist vegetarian cuisine) near Tenry\u016b-ji if possible. **Dinner:** Back in central Kyoto, try *Okonomiyaki* (savory pancake). |\\n| **Day 5** | **Philosopher's Path & Serenity** | Start at **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion)**. Walk the **Philosopher's Path** (approx. 2 km). Stop at quiet neighborhood temples along the way (e.g., Honen-in). Afternoon: Explore **Kodai-ji Temple** or visit the Kyoto Museum of Crafts and Design. | Ginkaku-ji (1.5 hrs). Philosopher's Path Walk (2 hrs, including stops). Afternoon Temple (2 hrs). | **Lunch:** Simple cafe or bakery along the Philosopher's Path. **Dinner:** Try local Kyoto-style *Ramen*. |\\n| **Day 6** | **Statues, Zen Complexes, and Craft** | Morning: Visit **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d** to see the 1001 statues of Kannon. Afternoon: Explore the massive **Daitoku-ji Temple** complex\u2014focus on a few sub-temples with famous gardens (like Koto-in). Evening: Attend a traditional cultural performance (e.g., Gion Corner, if interested in a broad overview). | Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d (1.5 hrs). Daitoku-ji Complex (3 hrs). Performance (1 hr). | **Dinner:** Splurge on *Kaiseki* (traditional multi-course haute cuisine) for a final cultural culinary experience (must book in advance). |\\n| **Day 7** | **Local Life & Departure** | Early morning visit to **Nishiki Market** for last-minute food souvenirs and a taste of local life (go early to beat crowds). Final temple visit: **Tofuku-ji Temple** (famous for its bridges and vibrant autumn colors). Head to the airport/next destination. | Market Visit (1.5 hrs). Tofuku-ji (1.5 hrs). Travel/Departure (Buffer time). | **Breakfast/Snacks:** Grab tasty street snacks at Nishiki Market. **Lunch:** Quick bite near Kyoto Station before departure. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 3. Budget Estimates (Per Person, Excluding International Flights)\\n\\nThese estimates are based on peak/shoulder season travel (Spring/Autumn). Kyoto can range from budget-friendly to extremely high-end depending on accommodation and dining choices.\\n\\n| Expense Category | Low Estimate (Hostel/Budget Hotel & Casual Dining) | Mid-Range Estimate (Mid-Tier Hotel & Mix of Dining) | Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Accommodation (6 Nights)** | \u00a536,000 (\u00a56,000/night) | \u00a578,000 (\u00a513,000/night) | Budget options are hostels/guesthouses; Mid-range includes comfortable 3-star hotels. |\\n| **Local Transportation** | \u00a56,500 | \u00a58,000 | Includes 3-day Bus Pass (\u00a52,200 total) + IC Card usage. Mid-range assumes more taxis/convenience. |\\n| **Entrance Fees/Activities** | \u00a512,000 | \u00a516,000 | Covers major temples (avg. \u00a5500-\u00a5600 each) plus Tea Ceremony (~\u00a54,000-\u00a55,000). |\\n| **Food & Drink (7 Days)** | \u00a535,000 (\u00a55,000/day) | \u00a563,000 (\u00a59,000/day) | Low-end relies on convenience stores, noodle shops. Mid-range allows for a few nicer dinners/Kaiseki lunch. |\\n| **Total Estimated Major Expenses (7 Days)** | **\u00a589,500 (approx. $575 USD)** | **\u00a5165,000 (approx. $1,065 USD)** | *Conversion rates fluctuate; use current rates.* |\\n\\n**Note on Budget:** This estimate excludes shopping, souvenirs, and any high-end cultural experiences (like private *Maiko* dinners).\\n\\n---\\n\\n### OPTIMIZATION TIPS: Money-Saving & Practical Advice\\n\\n1.  **Transportation Saving:**\\n    *   **Kyoto City Bus All-day Pass (\u00a5700):** Purchase this if you plan to take **three or more bus rides in one day**. Since most major sites rely on buses, this is invaluable. The pass is valid for unlimited travel on Kyoto City Buses.\\n    *   **IC Card Necessity:** Even with a bus pass, use an **IC Card (ICOCA, Suica, etc.)** for all train and subway travel. It saves fumbling for exact change at ticket machines.\\n    *   **Walking vs. Bus:** Days 1 and 5 (Higashiyama/Philosopher's Path) are best done almost entirely on foot to save money and experience the neighborhood feel between stops.\\n\\n2.  **Budget Dining Alternatives:**\\n    *   **Convenience Stores (Konbini):** 7-Eleven, Lawson, and FamilyMart offer surprisingly high-quality, cheap meals (onigiri, sandwiches, ready-made pasta, quality instant ramen) for breakfast or quick lunches (Budget: \u00a51,000/day).\\n    *   **Supermarkets:** Visit supermarkets (like Inoda Coffee branches or local chains) an hour before closing; they heavily discount pre-made bento boxes and sushi, perfect for a picnic dinner.\\n    *   **University Cafeterias:** If near a university area (like Kyoto University), their cafeterias often offer hearty, authentic meals for very low prices (\u00a5600-\u00a5900).\\n\\n3.  **Activity Saving:**\\n    *   **Free Sites:** While temples charge admission, take time in the free areas, such as the grounds of Fushimi Inari, the paths along the Kamo River, and the outer areas of Daitoku-ji.\\n    *   **Water Bottles:** Carry a reusable water bottle. While tap water is safe, many temples and public fountains offer purified drinking water stations, saving you money on bottled water, especially during hiking days (Day 3 & 4).\\n\\n### TRAVEL ESSENTIALS: Packing & Preparation\\n\\n1.  **Footwear is Crucial:** You will remove your shoes constantly (temples, tea houses, some restaurants). Pack **comfortable, easy-to-slip-on/off shoes**. Bring high-quality, blister-free walking shoes for the long days.\\n2.  **Layers (Especially for Autumn):** Daytime can be mild (18-22\u00b0C) but mornings and evenings drop significantly. Pack layers: a light thermal or turtleneck, a mid-layer fleece/sweater, and a waterproof/windproof outer shell jacket.\\n3.  **Temple Attire:** Pack one or two outfits that are modest (covering shoulders and knees) for your dedicated temple visits and the tea ceremony. Darker, muted colors are preferred for a cultural immersion feel.\\n4.  **Portable Power:** Bring a high-capacity power bank. Navigating with Google Maps/transport apps drains battery quickly, and you don't want to run out while trying to find your last bus connection.\\n5.  **Cash:** While major hotels and department stores accept cards, many smaller restaurants, bus drivers, entrance gates, and market stalls are **Cash Only (Yen)**. Ensure you withdraw enough cash upon arrival.\\n\\n### BACKUP PLANS: Weather and Unexpected Situations\\n\\n1.  **Rainy Day Backup (Heavy Rain/Typhoon):**\\n    *   **Substitute Day 4 (Arashiyama):** If rain makes the Bamboo Grove miserable, substitute Arashiyama for **Kyoto Station Area** activities: visit the stunning **Kyoto Station Building** architecture, explore the underground **Porta Mall**, or visit the **Kyoto National Museum** or the **Kyoto International Manga Museum**.\\n    *   **Indoor Cultural Immersion:** If it rains on Day 3, shift the Tea Ceremony earlier and pivot the afternoon to the **Kyoto Museum of Crafts and Design** (near Heian Shrine) or an indoor hands-on workshop (e.g., calligraphy or fan painting).\\n\\n2.  **Crowd/Closure Backup:**\\n    *   **Fushimi Inari Contingency (Day 3):** If Fushimi Inari is impossibly crowded at your preferred time, pivot to **Tofuku-ji Temple** (which you scheduled for Day 7) first. It offers beautiful scenery and is generally less packed early in the morning than Inari.\\n    *   **Nijo Castle Backup (Day 2):** If Nijo Castle has unexpectedly long lines, skip the interior and focus your time on the extensive, beautiful castle gardens, or substitute it with the smaller, atmospheric **Shoren-in Temple**.\\n\\n### LOCAL APPS, WEBSITES, & RESOURCES\\n\\n1.  **Google Maps:** Absolutely essential for navigation. It provides extremely accurate, real-time public transport schedules (bus numbers, platform locations, walking times). *Essential Download.*\\n2.  **Japan Transit Planner (or equivalent app like Jorudan):** While Google Maps is great for local transit, a dedicated transit app can sometimes offer better insight into long-distance train routing (useful if you decide to add a day trip to Nara or Osaka).\\n3.  **ICOCA App (or your region\u2019s equivalent):** If you have a compatible phone, you can load your IC Card digitally, making it easier to top up and use for travel/vending machines.\\n4.  **Japan National Tourism Organization (JNTO) Website:** Check this site prior to arrival for up-to-date information on any pandemic-related restrictions or regional closures, although this is generally less of a concern now.\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"model\"},\"intermediate_data\":{\"invocation_events\":[]},\"creation_timestamp\":1763640937.156543,\"rubrics\":null,\"app_details\":{\"agent_details\":{\"DestinationResearchAgent\":{\"name\":\"DestinationResearchAgent\",\"instructions\":\"\\n    You are a travel researcher. You will be given a destination and travel preferences, and you will research:\\n    - Best time to visit and weather patterns\\n    - Top attractions and must-see locations\\n    - Local culture, customs, and etiquette tips\\n    - Transportation options within the destination\\n    - Safety considerations and travel requirements\\n    Provide comprehensive destination insights for trip planning.\\n    \\n\\nYou are an agent. Your internal name is \\\"DestinationResearchAgent\\\". The description about you is \\\"An agent that researches travel destinations and gathers essential information\\\".\",\"tool_declarations\":[{\"function_declarations\":null,\"retrieval\":null,\"google_search_retrieval\":null,\"computer_use\":null,\"file_search\":null,\"code_execution\":null,\"enterprise_web_search\":null,\"google_maps\":null,\"google_search\":{\"exclude_domains\":null,\"blocking_confidence\":null,\"time_range_filter\":null},\"url_context\":null}]},\"ItineraryBuilderAgent\":{\"name\":\"ItineraryBuilderAgent\",\"instructions\":\"\\n    You are a professional travel planner. Using the research from \\\"destination_research\\\" output, create a detailed itinerary that includes:\\n    - Day-by-day schedule with recommended activities\\n    - Suggested accommodation areas or districts\\n    - Estimated time requirements for each activity\\n    - Meal recommendations and dining suggestions\\n    - Budget estimates for major expenses\\n    Structure it logically for easy following during the trip.\\n    \\n\\nYou are an agent. Your internal name is \\\"ItineraryBuilderAgent\\\". The description about you is \\\"An agent that creates structured travel itineraries with daily schedules\\\".\",\"tool_declarations\":[]},\"TravelOptimizerAgent\":{\"name\":\"TravelOptimizerAgent\",\"instructions\":\"\\n    You are a seasoned travel consultant. Using the itinerary from \\\"travel_itinerary\\\" output, optimize it by adding:\\n    - Money-saving tips and budget alternatives\\n    - Packing recommendations specific to the destination\\n    - Backup plans for weather or unexpected situations\\n    - Local apps, websites, or resources to download\\n    - Cultural do's and don'ts for respectful travel\\n\\n    Format the final output as:\\n    \\n    ITINERARY: ## 7-Day Kyoto Cultural Deep-Dive Itinerary\\n\\nThis itinerary is designed for a traveler seeking a rich cultural immersion, focusing on Kyoto's temples, traditional arts (tea ceremony), and natural beauty. It assumes an optimal travel period of Autumn (October/November) for comfortable weather and scenery.\\n\\n### 1. Suggested Accommodation Areas\\n\\nFor a cultural deep-dive, staying centrally or near major cultural hubs is ideal for minimizing travel time.\\n\\n*   **Gion/Higashiyama:** Perfect for atmosphere, proximity to Kiyomizu-dera, Sannenzaka, and easy access to traditional restaurants and tea houses. **(Recommended for Atmosphere)**\\n*   **Downtown Kyoto (Kawaramachi/Shijo Area):** Excellent for convenience, transportation hub (bus/subway), shopping, and modern dining options, while still being a short bus ride from major sites. **(Recommended for Convenience)**\\n*   **Near Kyoto Station:** Best if you plan multiple day trips (e.g., Nara), but the area is modern and less atmospheric for evening strolls.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2. Detailed Daily Itinerary\\n\\n| Day | Focus Area & Location | Recommended Activities | Time Estimate | Meal Recommendations & Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Day 1** | **Arrival & Southern Higashiyama** | Arrive, check into accommodation, settle in. Visit **Kiyomizu-Dera Temple** (aim for late afternoon light). Walk down **Sannenzaka & Ninenzaka** preserved streets. Evening: Discreet exploration of **Gion District** (Hanamikoji Street). | Travel/Check-in (3 hrs). Kiyomizu-dera (1.5 hrs). Streets/Shopping (1.5 hrs). Gion Stroll (1 hr). | **Dinner:** Seek out a traditional *kappo* (counter dining) experience in Gion or Pontocho Alley. **Cultural Tip:** Wear comfortable slip-ons for temples. |\\n| **Day 2** | **The Golden North & Zen Contemplation** | Morning visit to **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)** (arrive shortly after opening). Travel south to **Ryoan-ji Temple** for quiet contemplation of the Zen rock garden. Afternoon: Visit **Nijo-jo Castle** (Nightingale floors). | Kinkaku-ji (1.5 hrs). Travel (0.5 hr). Ryoan-ji (1 hr). Nijo-jo Castle (2 hrs). | **Lunch:** Try *Yudofu* (simmered tofu) near Ryoan-ji, a local vegetarian specialty. **Transport:** Bus heavy day. Purchase a Kyoto Bus Pass. |\\n| **Day 3** | **Torii Gates & Traditional Arts** | **Fushimi Inari Taisha:** Very early start (before 8:00 AM) to hike through the *torii* gates with fewer crowds. Midday: Return to central Kyoto. Afternoon: **Pre-booked Traditional Tea Ceremony** experience. | Fushimi Inari Hike (3-4 hrs). Travel/Rest (1 hr). Tea Ceremony (1.5 hrs). | **Lunch:** Inari area street food (e.g., *Inari Sushi*). **Dinner:** Explore the vibrant food stalls and restaurants around Nishiki Market (though the market closes early). |\\n| **Day 4** | **Western Nature Immersion (Arashiyama)** | Morning travel to **Arashiyama**. Visit **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple** and its magnificent landscape garden (UNESCO). Walk through the serene **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove**. Optional: Boat ride on the Hozugawa River or Sagano Scenic Railway (seasonal). | Travel (1 hr round trip). Tenry\u016b-ji (2 hrs). Bamboo Grove (1 hr). Optional Activity (2 hrs). | **Lunch:** Enjoy *Shojin Ryori* (Buddhist vegetarian cuisine) near Tenry\u016b-ji if possible. **Dinner:** Back in central Kyoto, try *Okonomiyaki* (savory pancake). |\\n| **Day 5** | **Philosopher's Path & Serenity** | Start at **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion)**. Walk the **Philosopher's Path** (approx. 2 km). Stop at quiet neighborhood temples along the way (e.g., Honen-in). Afternoon: Explore **Kodai-ji Temple** or visit the Kyoto Museum of Crafts and Design. | Ginkaku-ji (1.5 hrs). Philosopher's Path Walk (2 hrs, including stops). Afternoon Temple (2 hrs). | **Lunch:** Simple cafe or bakery along the Philosopher's Path. **Dinner:** Try local Kyoto-style *Ramen*. |\\n| **Day 6** | **Statues, Zen Complexes, and Craft** | Morning: Visit **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d** to see the 1001 statues of Kannon. Afternoon: Explore the massive **Daitoku-ji Temple** complex\u2014focus on a few sub-temples with famous gardens (like Koto-in). Evening: Attend a traditional cultural performance (e.g., Gion Corner, if interested in a broad overview). | Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d (1.5 hrs). Daitoku-ji Complex (3 hrs). Performance (1 hr). | **Dinner:** Splurge on *Kaiseki* (traditional multi-course haute cuisine) for a final cultural culinary experience (must book in advance). |\\n| **Day 7** | **Local Life & Departure** | Early morning visit to **Nishiki Market** for last-minute food souvenirs and a taste of local life (go early to beat crowds). Final temple visit: **Tofuku-ji Temple** (famous for its bridges and vibrant autumn colors). Head to the airport/next destination. | Market Visit (1.5 hrs). Tofuku-ji (1.5 hrs). Travel/Departure (Buffer time). | **Breakfast/Snacks:** Grab tasty street snacks at Nishiki Market. **Lunch:** Quick bite near Kyoto Station before departure. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 3. Budget Estimates (Per Person, Excluding International Flights)\\n\\nThese estimates are based on peak/shoulder season travel (Spring/Autumn). Kyoto can range from budget-friendly to extremely high-end depending on accommodation and dining choices.\\n\\n| Expense Category | Low Estimate (Hostel/Budget Hotel & Casual Dining) | Mid-Range Estimate (Mid-Tier Hotel & Mix of Dining) | Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Accommodation (6 Nights)** | \u00a536,000 (\u00a56,000/night) | \u00a578,000 (\u00a513,000/night) | Budget options are hostels/guesthouses; Mid-range includes comfortable 3-star hotels. |\\n| **Local Transportation** | \u00a56,500 | \u00a58,000 | Includes 3-day Bus Pass (\u00a52,200 total) + IC Card usage. Mid-range assumes more taxis/convenience. |\\n| **Entrance Fees/Activities** | \u00a512,000 | \u00a516,000 | Covers major temples (avg. \u00a5500-\u00a5600 each) plus Tea Ceremony (~\u00a54,000-\u00a55,000). |\\n| **Food & Drink (7 Days)** | \u00a535,000 (\u00a55,000/day) | \u00a563,000 (\u00a59,000/day) | Low-end relies on convenience stores, noodle shops. Mid-range allows for a few nicer dinners/Kaiseki lunch. |\\n| **Total Estimated Major Expenses (7 Days)** | **\u00a589,500 (approx. $575 USD)** | **\u00a5165,000 (approx. $1,065 USD)** | *Conversion rates fluctuate; use current rates.* |\\n\\n**Note on Budget:** This estimate excludes shopping, souvenirs, and any high-end cultural experiences (like private *Maiko* dinners).\\n\\n    OPTIMIZATION TIPS: [your money-saving and practical tips here]\\n\\n    TRAVEL ESSENTIALS: [packing and preparation advice here]\\n\\n    BACKUP PLANS: [alternative options and contingencies here]\\n    \\n\\nYou are an agent. Your internal name is \\\"TravelOptimizerAgent\\\". The description about you is \\\"An agent that optimizes travel plans with practical advice and alternatives\\\".\",\"tool_declarations\":[]}}}},\"expected_invocation\":{\"invocation_id\":\"e-f2ef503f-ae2d-4fe2-b8d4-40c7327c0051\",\"user_content\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"Plan a 7-day cultural deep-dive travel experience in Kyoto focusing on temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature.\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"user\"},\"final_response\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"As your TravelOptimizerAgent, I have integrated crucial money-saving strategies, specific packing lists, contingency plans, and essential local resources into your detailed 7-Day Kyoto Cultural Deep-Dive Itinerary.\\n\\n# 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive Itinerary: Kyoto (Optimized)\\n\\nThis itinerary is designed for a comprehensive cultural immersion in Kyoto, balancing iconic historical sites with intimate experiences of Zen, tea traditions, and natural beauty.\\n\\n**Best Time to Travel:** Spring (Cherry Blossoms) or Autumn (Fall Foliage).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Suggested Accommodation Area\\n\\nFor a first-time deep dive focusing on culture and accessibility, consider staying in or near:\\n\\n1.  **Higashiyama (East Kyoto):** Excellent access to Kiyomizu-dera, Gion, and the Philosopher's Path. Very atmospheric.\\n2.  **Central Kyoto (Near Karasuma/Kawaramachi Stations):** Best for transportation links (subway and buses) and modern dining/shopping convenience.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Day-by-Day Schedule (With Optimizations)\\n\\n### Day 1: Arrival & The Iconic East (Higashiyama)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Optimization Notes & Budget Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | Arrival at KIX/ITM, Transfer to Kyoto, Check-in | **BUDGET TIP:** If using KIX, the *Haruka Express* is fast but expensive. Consider the cheaper **JR Local Rapid Service** followed by a transfer, or the **Airport Limousine Bus** for direct, often cheaper service to Kyoto Station. |\\n| Lunch | Simple Udon or Soba near your hotel. | **MONEY SAVER:** Avoid restaurants directly facing major tourist spots (like the street leading to Kiyomizu-dera). Walk 2-3 blocks away for better value. |\\n| Afternoon | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple** | **CULTURAL DO\u2019s/DON\u2019Ts:** Use the purification fountain (Temizuya) correctly: ladle water, wash left hand, then right, pour water into your cupped left hand to rinse the ladle handle, then discard water. |\\n| Late Afternoon | Stroll **Sannenzaka & Ninenzaka** streets | **PACKING:** Wear comfortable, slip-on shoes. You will encounter many steep, uneven stone steps and some shops/temples require shoe removal. |\\n| Dinner | **Higashiyama Dinner** | **BACKUP:** If Pontocho/Gion is booked, try the dining options inside the Shijo Kawaramachi department stores (like Takashimaya or Daimaru) for high-quality but cheaper set menus at lunchtime or early dinner. |\\n\\n### Day 2: Zen, Tea, and Geiko Culture\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Optimization Notes & Budget Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | **Traditional Tea Ceremony Experience** | **MONEY SAVER:** Book a group ceremony rather than a private one. Look for ceremonies held in public community centers or smaller temples rather than high-end tourist venues for savings of 30-50%. |\\n| Lunch | Lunch in Gion | **LOCAL APP:** Use **Tabelog** (Japan's primary restaurant review site) to find highly-rated, lower-priced lunch sets (*teishoku*) in the Gion area. |\\n| Afternoon | **Kodai-ji Temple** | **CULTURAL DON\u2019T:** Do not use flash photography anywhere inside temple buildings, even if it's not explicitly forbidden, as it degrades the art and disturbs visitors. |\\n| Evening | **Gion District Walk** | **CRUCIAL DON'T:** Never attempt to stop, touch, or photograph a Geiko/Maiko if they are clearly rushing. Respect their work commute. Stay on public sidewalks. |\\n| Dinner | **Pontocho Alley Dining** | **BACKUP PLAN:** If Pontocho is too busy or expensive, explore the smaller side alleys branching off Shijo Dori for less crowded *yakitori* joints. |\\n\\n### Day 3: Golden Splendor and Contemplation\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Optimization Notes & Budget Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)** | **MONEY SAVER:** Entry fees are fixed, but arriving right at opening (9 AM) avoids paying for entry when the sun is harsh and crowds are thick, maximizing the visual experience for the standard fee. |\\n| Transit | Travel to Ryoan-ji | **TRANSPORT TIP:** Purchase an **ICOCA/Suica Card** immediately. This refillable card streamlines bus/subway travel immensely\u2014no need to fumble for exact change or bus passes daily. |\\n| Afternoon | **Ryoan-ji Temple** | **CULTURAL DO:** Sit quietly for at least 10 minutes at the viewing platform. The garden is designed for quiet contemplation; rushing it defeats the purpose of this Zen stop. |\\n| Late Afternoon | **Nishijin Textile Center (Optional)** | **BUDGET ALTERNATIVE:** If skipping the center, visit a small, independent craft shop in the Nishijin area to see artisans at work without the tourist shop markup. |\\n| Dinner | **Central Kyoto/Nishiki Market Area** | **MONEY SAVER:** Nishiki Market vendors often sell ready-to-eat foods (pickles, mochi, skewers). Grab a selection of these for a delicious, varied, and cheap dinner feast. |\\n\\n### Day 4: The Thousand Gates of Inari\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Optimization Notes & Budget Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | **Fushimi Inari Taisha Shrine** | **BACKUP PLAN (Heat/Rain):** If hiking in extreme heat or heavy rain, only go up to the first major junction (Yotsutsuji intersection) for the famous view, then descend. The upper mountain paths can be exposed. |\\n| Lunch | **Inari Street Food** | **CULTURAL DO:** Trying *Kitsune Udon* (udon noodles with sweet fried tofu) is highly recommended here, as the fox (*kitsune*) is the messenger of the Inari deity. |\\n| Afternoon | Return to Central Kyoto & Rest | **PACKING:** Bring moisture-wicking socks and a small towel. You will sweat while hiking Inari, and local washrooms/sento often charge for towel rental. |\\n| Evening | **Local Izakaya Experience** | **CULTURAL DO:** When ordering at an Izakaya, expect a small cover charge called *ot\u014dshi* or *tsukidashi*\u2014this is mandatory and includes a small appetizer. |\\n\\n### Day 5: Arashiyama Nature Immersion\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Optimization Notes & Budget Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | Travel to Arashiyama & **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple** | **MONEY SAVER:** Tenry\u016b-ji has two entry fees: the main hall/garden and the separate Bamboo Grove access. If you are on a tight budget, skip the main hall but walk through the famous **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove** (Day 5 PM activity) which is free to access from the back paths. |\\n| Lunch | Riverside Dining | **BUDGET TIP:** Buy high-quality *bento* boxes from a department store basement (*depachika*) near Kyoto Station before heading out, and have a scenic picnic lunch by the river. |\\n| Afternoon | **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove** | **LOCAL RESOURCE:** Download an offline map of Arashiyama. The paths diverge, and good signage is sometimes lacking deeper in the woods. |\\n| Evening | **Relaxation / Onsen** | **PACKING/CULTURAL NOTE:** If visiting a public bath/onsen, you must be comfortable bathing nude (separately for men/women). No tattoos are allowed in many traditional establishments\u2014research tattoo-friendly options like **Tendon no Yu** if this is a concern. |\\n\\n### Day 6: Shogun Power and Secluded Zen\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Optimization Notes & Budget Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | **Nijo-jo Castle** | **CULTURAL DON'T:** Do not step on the edges of the \\\"nightingale floors\\\" as they are more likely to squeak when stepped on near the perimeter. Walk carefully in the center if possible. |\\n| Afternoon | **Daitoku-ji Temple Complex** | **ZEN TIP:** Select *one* sub-temple (e.g., Koto-in) to focus on deeply, as paying entry fees for too many small temples drains the budget quickly. The larger Daitoku-ji grounds are free to wander. |\\n| Evening | **Philosopher's Path (Tetsugaku-no-michi) Walk** | **BACKUP PLAN (Rain):** If it is raining heavily, substitute this walk with a visit to the **Kyoto Museum of Crafts and Design** near Miyako Messe for an indoor cultural experience. |\\n| Dinner | **Near Demachiyanagi or Imadegawa** | **MONEY SAVER:** This university-adjacent area (near Kyoto University) has many excellent, cheap, and authentic restaurants catering to students. |\\n\\n### Day 7: Final Masterpieces & Departure\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Optimization Notes & Budget Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d Temple** | **CULTURAL DO:** Photography is strictly forbidden inside. Before entering, pause outside to meditate on the sheer scale of the statues; this helps prepare your mind for the overwhelming experience inside. |\\n| Midday | **Final Reflection Spot (Honen-in Temple)** | **PACKING:** Pick up high-quality, long-lasting souvenirs like Kyoto incense (*Oko*) or preserved tea leaves today rather than waiting until the station. |\\n| Lunch | **Kyoto Station Area** | **BUDGET TIP:** Head to the Isetan department store food halls (*depachika*) around 1 PM. Some high-end deli/bakery items are deeply discounted near closing time (usually 6 PM onwards, check local schedules). |\\n| Afternoon | Souvenir Shopping & Departure Prep | **LOCAL APP:** Use **Google Maps** for real-time navigation to the airport transfer point, confirming the final platform/gate number for the Haruka Express or Shinkansen. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Optimization Deep Dive\\n\\n### \ud83d\udcb0 Money-Saving Tips & Budget Alternatives\\n\\n1.  **Transportation Flexibility:** Rely primarily on walking and the efficient bus network. Purchase the **Kyoto City Bus One-Day Pass (700 JPY)** only if you plan 3 or more bus rides. For days using the JR lines (Inari, Arashiyama), an ICOCA/Suica card is best.\\n2.  **Temple Passes:** Most major temples charge between 400\u2013600 JPY entry. If you find yourself consistently skipping afternoon visits due to fatigue or budget strain, prioritize only the UNESCO sites (Kiyomizu, Kinkaku-ji, Tenryu-ji) to save entry fees.\\n3.  **Dining Strategy:** Embrace *Konbini* (convenience stores like 7-Eleven, Lawson, Family Mart) for excellent, fresh breakfasts (Onigiri, Sandwiches) for under 400 JPY. For dinner, prioritize lunch sets (*teishoku*) which offer the same quality as dinner but at half the price.\\n4.  **Water:** Carry a reusable water bottle. Vending machines are everywhere (and cheaper than buying bottled water in stores), and many temples have public water sources for refilling (check signage).\\n\\n### \ud83e\uddf3 Packing Recommendations (Kyoto Specific)\\n\\n*   **Comfortable Footwear:** Non-negotiable. You will easily walk 8-10 miles daily over uneven paving, gravel, and stairs.\\n*   **Layered Clothing:** Evenings can be cool, especially near the rivers or when entering shady temple halls, even if the day is warm. Pack light, breathable layers.\\n*   **Small, Foldable Umbrella/Rain Shell:** Essential, especially during the rainy season (June) or unpredictable shoulder seasons.\\n*   **Small Gift (Omiyage):** If visiting a private tea ceremony or seeking specific artisanal services, having a small, attractively packaged souvenir from your home country (or a standard Japanese gift item) is a very polite gesture.\\n*   **Portable Charger:** Early mornings and late evenings mean relying heavily on navigation apps; keep your phone charged.\\n\\n### \ud83c\udf27\ufe0f Backup Plans for Weather or Unexpected Situations\\n\\n| Situation | Backup Activity / Contingency Plan |\\n| :--- | :--- |\\n| **Heavy Rain/Typhoon Warning** | **Day 5/6 Substitute:** Visit the **Kyoto International Manga Museum** (Central Kyoto), explore the massive underground shopping malls around Kyoto Station, or spend a long afternoon in the **Kyoto National Museum** or the **Kyoto Museum of Crafts and Design**. |\\n| **Extreme Heat (Summer)** | Focus on sites with excellent air conditioning (Nijo-jo, Museums). Schedule Fushimi Inari for pre-sunrise (5:30 AM start) and retreat indoors between 11 AM and 3 PM. Utilize public transport heavily. |\\n| **Unexpected Temple Closure** | Many small temples close for maintenance or private ceremonies. **Backup Plan:** Head to the nearby **Shinto Shrines** (which often have different opening hours) or explore local, unnamed neighborhood temples\u2014these are often the most authentic finds. |\\n| **Transportation Strikes/Delays** | If the JR line to Arashiyama is delayed, use the **Randen Tram** instead. It\u2019s slower but offers a lovely, scenic ride through older districts. |\\n\\n### \ud83d\udcf1 Local Apps, Websites, or Resources to Download\\n\\n1.  **Google Maps:** Essential for bus routes, walking times, and train schedules. Turn on \\\"Public Transit\\\" layers.\\n2.  **Japan Transit Planner (Jorudan) or Navitime:** Excellent secondary resources for complex train routes involving multiple carriers (e.g., Shinkansen connections).\\n3.  **Tabelog:** For reliable restaurant reviews and finding local gems away from tourist traps.\\n4.  **Google Translate:** Download the Japanese language pack for offline translation, particularly useful for reading menus or signs.\\n5.  **ICOCA/Suica App:** If you use a digital wallet on your phone, you can load and manage your transport card digitally, eliminating the need for a physical card.\\n\\n### \u26e9\ufe0f Cultural Do's & Don'ts for Respectful Travel\\n\\n| Do | Don't |\\n| :--- | :--- |\\n| **DO** use quiet, respectful tones inside temples, shrines, and on public transport. | **DON'T** eat, drink, or smoke while walking down the historic streets (Sannenzaka, Gion). Find a designated spot. |\\n| **DO** learn a few key phrases: *Arigatou gozaimasu* (Thank you very much) and *Sumimasen* (Excuse me/Sorry). | **DON'T** take pictures of people without asking, especially Geiko/Maiko or people performing religious rites. |\\n| **DO** accept business cards (if exchanged) with both hands and examine them briefly before putting them away respectfully. | **DON'T** leave trash behind. Carry a small plastic bag for wrappers and bottles until you find a trash can. |\\n| **DO** remove your shoes promptly when entering homes, certain temples, and traditional restaurants/tea houses. | **DON'T** point with your index finger; use an open hand gesture instead. |\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"model\"},\"intermediate_data\":{\"tool_uses\":[],\"tool_responses\":[],\"intermediate_responses\":[]},\"creation_timestamp\":1763640594.995979,\"rubrics\":null,\"app_details\":null},\"eval_metric_results\":[{\"metric_name\":\"tool_trajectory_avg_score\",\"threshold\":0.5,\"judge_model_options\":null,\"criterion\":null,\"score\":1.0,\"eval_status\":1,\"details\":{\"rubric_scores\":null}},{\"metric_name\":\"response_match_score\",\"threshold\":0.5,\"judge_model_options\":null,\"criterion\":null,\"score\":0.5769442912300055,\"eval_status\":1,\"details\":{\"rubric_scores\":null}}]}],\"session_id\":\"___eval___session___25bc9e80-f2ee-43a1-8f23-b95b7a491b3a\",\"session_details\":{\"id\":\"___eval___session___25bc9e80-f2ee-43a1-8f23-b95b7a491b3a\",\"app_name\":\"TravelPlannerAgent\",\"user_id\":\"user\",\"state\":{\"travel_itinerary\":\"## 7-Day Kyoto Cultural Deep-Dive Itinerary\\n\\nThis itinerary is designed for a traveler seeking a rich cultural immersion, focusing on Kyoto's temples, traditional arts (tea ceremony), and natural beauty. It assumes an optimal travel period of Autumn (October/November) for comfortable weather and scenery.\\n\\n### 1. Suggested Accommodation Areas\\n\\nFor a cultural deep-dive, staying centrally or near major cultural hubs is ideal for minimizing travel time.\\n\\n*   **Gion/Higashiyama:** Perfect for atmosphere, proximity to Kiyomizu-dera, Sannenzaka, and easy access to traditional restaurants and tea houses. **(Recommended for Atmosphere)**\\n*   **Downtown Kyoto (Kawaramachi/Shijo Area):** Excellent for convenience, transportation hub (bus/subway), shopping, and modern dining options, while still being a short bus ride from major sites. **(Recommended for Convenience)**\\n*   **Near Kyoto Station:** Best if you plan multiple day trips (e.g., Nara), but the area is modern and less atmospheric for evening strolls.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2. Detailed Daily Itinerary\\n\\n| Day | Focus Area & Location | Recommended Activities | Time Estimate | Meal Recommendations & Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Day 1** | **Arrival & Southern Higashiyama** | Arrive, check into accommodation, settle in. Visit **Kiyomizu-Dera Temple** (aim for late afternoon light). Walk down **Sannenzaka & Ninenzaka** preserved streets. Evening: Discreet exploration of **Gion District** (Hanamikoji Street). | Travel/Check-in (3 hrs). Kiyomizu-dera (1.5 hrs). Streets/Shopping (1.5 hrs). Gion Stroll (1 hr). | **Dinner:** Seek out a traditional *kappo* (counter dining) experience in Gion or Pontocho Alley. **Cultural Tip:** Wear comfortable slip-ons for temples. |\\n| **Day 2** | **The Golden North & Zen Contemplation** | Morning visit to **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)** (arrive shortly after opening). Travel south to **Ryoan-ji Temple** for quiet contemplation of the Zen rock garden. Afternoon: Visit **Nijo-jo Castle** (Nightingale floors). | Kinkaku-ji (1.5 hrs). Travel (0.5 hr). Ryoan-ji (1 hr). Nijo-jo Castle (2 hrs). | **Lunch:** Try *Yudofu* (simmered tofu) near Ryoan-ji, a local vegetarian specialty. **Transport:** Bus heavy day. Purchase a Kyoto Bus Pass. |\\n| **Day 3** | **Torii Gates & Traditional Arts** | **Fushimi Inari Taisha:** Very early start (before 8:00 AM) to hike through the *torii* gates with fewer crowds. Midday: Return to central Kyoto. Afternoon: **Pre-booked Traditional Tea Ceremony** experience. | Fushimi Inari Hike (3-4 hrs). Travel/Rest (1 hr). Tea Ceremony (1.5 hrs). | **Lunch:** Inari area street food (e.g., *Inari Sushi*). **Dinner:** Explore the vibrant food stalls and restaurants around Nishiki Market (though the market closes early). |\\n| **Day 4** | **Western Nature Immersion (Arashiyama)** | Morning travel to **Arashiyama**. Visit **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple** and its magnificent landscape garden (UNESCO). Walk through the serene **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove**. Optional: Boat ride on the Hozugawa River or Sagano Scenic Railway (seasonal). | Travel (1 hr round trip). Tenry\u016b-ji (2 hrs). Bamboo Grove (1 hr). Optional Activity (2 hrs). | **Lunch:** Enjoy *Shojin Ryori* (Buddhist vegetarian cuisine) near Tenry\u016b-ji if possible. **Dinner:** Back in central Kyoto, try *Okonomiyaki* (savory pancake). |\\n| **Day 5** | **Philosopher's Path & Serenity** | Start at **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion)**. Walk the **Philosopher's Path** (approx. 2 km). Stop at quiet neighborhood temples along the way (e.g., Honen-in). Afternoon: Explore **Kodai-ji Temple** or visit the Kyoto Museum of Crafts and Design. | Ginkaku-ji (1.5 hrs). Philosopher's Path Walk (2 hrs, including stops). Afternoon Temple (2 hrs). | **Lunch:** Simple cafe or bakery along the Philosopher's Path. **Dinner:** Try local Kyoto-style *Ramen*. |\\n| **Day 6** | **Statues, Zen Complexes, and Craft** | Morning: Visit **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d** to see the 1001 statues of Kannon. Afternoon: Explore the massive **Daitoku-ji Temple** complex\u2014focus on a few sub-temples with famous gardens (like Koto-in). Evening: Attend a traditional cultural performance (e.g., Gion Corner, if interested in a broad overview). | Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d (1.5 hrs). Daitoku-ji Complex (3 hrs). Performance (1 hr). | **Dinner:** Splurge on *Kaiseki* (traditional multi-course haute cuisine) for a final cultural culinary experience (must book in advance). |\\n| **Day 7** | **Local Life & Departure** | Early morning visit to **Nishiki Market** for last-minute food souvenirs and a taste of local life (go early to beat crowds). Final temple visit: **Tofuku-ji Temple** (famous for its bridges and vibrant autumn colors). Head to the airport/next destination. | Market Visit (1.5 hrs). Tofuku-ji (1.5 hrs). Travel/Departure (Buffer time). | **Breakfast/Snacks:** Grab tasty street snacks at Nishiki Market. **Lunch:** Quick bite near Kyoto Station before departure. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 3. Budget Estimates (Per Person, Excluding International Flights)\\n\\nThese estimates are based on peak/shoulder season travel (Spring/Autumn). Kyoto can range from budget-friendly to extremely high-end depending on accommodation and dining choices.\\n\\n| Expense Category | Low Estimate (Hostel/Budget Hotel & Casual Dining) | Mid-Range Estimate (Mid-Tier Hotel & Mix of Dining) | Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Accommodation (6 Nights)** | \u00a536,000 (\u00a56,000/night) | \u00a578,000 (\u00a513,000/night) | Budget options are hostels/guesthouses; Mid-range includes comfortable 3-star hotels. |\\n| **Local Transportation** | \u00a56,500 | \u00a58,000 | Includes 3-day Bus Pass (\u00a52,200 total) + IC Card usage. Mid-range assumes more taxis/convenience. |\\n| **Entrance Fees/Activities** | \u00a512,000 | \u00a516,000 | Covers major temples (avg. \u00a5500-\u00a5600 each) plus Tea Ceremony (~\u00a54,000-\u00a55,000). |\\n| **Food & Drink (7 Days)** | \u00a535,000 (\u00a55,000/day) | \u00a563,000 (\u00a59,000/day) | Low-end relies on convenience stores, noodle shops. Mid-range allows for a few nicer dinners/Kaiseki lunch. |\\n| **Total Estimated Major Expenses (7 Days)** | **\u00a589,500 (approx. $575 USD)** | **\u00a5165,000 (approx. $1,065 USD)** | *Conversion rates fluctuate; use current rates.* |\\n\\n**Note on Budget:** This estimate excludes shopping, souvenirs, and any high-end cultural experiences (like private *Maiko* dinners).\",\"__llm_request_key__\":\"ae1372c2-9fd6-4687-a9fd-3223c5c6661c\",\"destination_research\":\"Based on your request for a 7-day cultural deep-dive in Kyoto focusing on temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature, here is a comprehensive plan covering the best time to visit, key sights, cultural tips, transportation, and safety.\\n\\n***\\n\\n## Kyoto Cultural Deep-Dive: 7-Day Itinerary Planner\\n\\n### 1. Best Time to Visit and Weather Patterns\\n\\nThe best times to visit Kyoto for pleasant weather and beautiful scenery are **Spring (March to May)** and **Autumn (October to November)**.\\n\\n*   **Spring (March - May):** Ideal for viewing the famous cherry blossoms, typically from late March to early April. Weather is generally warm and comfortable, though rain is possible, especially towards late May.\\n*   **Autumn (October - November):** Considered one of the best times with lovely fall foliage peaking in mid- to late November. Weather is cool and comfortable, often sunny with dry air.\\n*   **Summer (June - August):** Hot and humid, with a rainy season from mid-June to late July.\\n*   **Winter (December - February):** Cold, with occasional snow, offering a more serene atmosphere.\\n\\n**Recommendation:** Aim for **October or November** for the best combination of comfortable sightseeing weather and stunning natural beauty to complement your temple visits. Be aware that spring and autumn are peak seasons, meaning larger crowds and higher prices.\\n\\n### 2. Top Attractions & Must-See Locations (Cultural Focus)\\n\\nKyoto has over 2,000 religious structures, including 17 UNESCO World Heritage Sites. For a cultural deep-dive, prioritize these:\\n\\n| Theme | Location | Key Feature/Cultural Significance |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Iconic Temples** | **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)** | A UNESCO World Heritage Site, famous for its top two floors covered in gold leaf, reflected in a pond. |\\n| **Zen & Nature** | **Ryoan-ji Temple** | Home to a world-famous Zen rock garden. |\\n| **Pilgrimage & Views** | **Kiyomizu-Dera Temple** | A revered World Heritage site known for its massive wooden veranda built without nails, offering views over Kyoto. |\\n| **Nature & Shinto** | **Fushimi Inari Taisha Shrine** | Famous for the thousands of vibrant red *torii* gates lining the hiking trails up the mountain. |\\n| **Gardens & Bamboo** | **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple** | A leading Zen temple in Arashiyama, known for its ancient and celebrated landscape garden (UNESCO site). |\\n| **Tradition & History** | **Gion District** | Kyoto\u2019s traditional entertainment district, famous for its preserved *machiya* houses and the chance to spot *Maiko* (apprentice geisha). |\\n| **Nature/Gardens** | **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove** | A serene area near Tenry\u016b-ji, perfect for a nature walk. |\\n\\n### 3. Local Culture, Customs, and Etiquette Tips\\n\\nKyoto highly values tradition and respect. Observing local etiquette will greatly enhance your experience.\\n\\n*   **Temples and Shrines:**\\n    *   **Respectful Attire:** Dress conservatively, covering shoulders, chest, and knees. Avoid very bright or flashy colors.\\n    *   **Footwear:** Be prepared to remove your shoes before entering temple buildings or tea rooms (*tatami* areas). Slip-on shoes are convenient.\\n    *   **Conduct:** Maintain quiet voices; temples are for prayer and worship.\\n    *   **Photography:** Observe signs carefully; photography is often prohibited inside buildings or with flash. Do not photograph worshippers without permission.\\n    *   **Purification Ritual (*Temizuya*):** If you use the water fountain, use the ladle to cleanse your left hand, then your right, then rinse your mouth (pouring water into your cupped left hand\u2014never touch the ladle to your lips), and finally rinse the ladle handle.\\n    *   **Bowing:** A slight bow when entering grounds is a sign of respect.\\n*   **General Etiquette:**\\n    *   **Tipping:** Tipping is **not customary** and is generally not appreciated. A polite \\\"Okini\\\" (thank you in Kansai dialect) is sufficient.\\n    *   **Noise:** Keep noise levels down, especially in sacred areas and on public transport.\\n    *   **Eating/Drinking:** Abstain from eating or drinking in sacred areas like temples and shrines.\\n    *   **Queuing:** Always line up in an organized queue when waiting for buses, trains, or shops.\\n    *   **Maiko/Geiko:** Do not bother, crowd, or touch *Maiko* (apprentice geisha), especially in the Gion area. They are busy and may not have time for photos.\\n*   **Tea Ceremony:** Be mindful of specific tea ceremony etiquette, such as handling the bowl and consuming the tea/sweets as instructed by your host. [This will be covered during the booking.]\\n\\n### 4. Transportation Options\\n\\nKyoto's public transport is extensive but can be somewhat complex as attractions are often not near subway stations.\\n\\n*   **Buses (Recommended for Tourists):** The **bus network is dense** and is generally considered the **best way to reach most major tourist attractions** like Kinkaku-ji and Kiyomizu-dera.\\n    *   **Payment:** Pay a fixed fare (cash only, exact change or 1000-yen notes accepted). You enter at the back and exit at the front. Consider a **Kyoto Bus Pass** for unlimited rides if you plan many stops.\\n*   **Subway & Trains:** Kyoto has two subway lines (Karasuma N-S, Tozai E-W) that are good for traveling north-south or east-west across the city center. Trains are useful for day trips (e.g., to Arashiyama, Nara).\\n*   **IC Cards:** Use an IC Card (like ICOCA, Suica, PASMO) for easy tap-and-go payment on virtually all trains and buses.\\n*   **Walking/Cycling:** The city center is relatively flat and excellent for walking or renting a bicycle for local exploration.\\n*   **Taxis:** Plentiful but more expensive; use them when public transport stops (around 10:30 PM) or for convenience. Most accept cash only.\\n\\n### 5. Safety Considerations and Travel Requirements\\n\\nKyoto is known as one of the safest cities in the world.\\n\\n*   **General Safety:** Crime rates are low, and lost items are often returned. Exercise common sense: be alert in crowded markets (like Nishiki Market) and avoid poorly lit paths at night, especially as a solo traveler.\\n*   **Traffic:** Be cautious of **bicycles on sidewalks**; if you hear a bell, move aside.\\n*   **Emergency Numbers:** Dial **110 for Police** and **119 for Ambulance/Fire**.\\n*   **Natural Disasters:** Be aware of the risk of earthquakes or typhoons. Prepare by having paper maps/contact info, extra medication, and knowing the location of the nearest Emergency Evacuation Site.\\n*   **Travel Requirements:** Check current visa/entry requirements for Japan well in advance. Tap water is clean.\\n\\n***\\n\\n## Suggested 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive Structure\\n\\nThis framework integrates your interests:\\n\\n| Day | Focus Area | Suggested Activities & Culture Immersion |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **1** | **Arrival & Gion/Higashiyama South** | Check into accommodation. Explore **Kiyomizu-Dera Temple** (aim for late afternoon/sunset). Walk the preserved streets of Sannenzaka and Ninenzaka. Evening exploration of **Gion** (respectfully observe, do not bother *Maiko*). |\\n| **2** | **Zen & The Golden North** | Morning visit to **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)**. Midday visit to **Ryoan-ji Temple** (Zen rock garden contemplation). Afternoon: Visit **Nijo-jo Castle** for historical architecture. |\\n| **3** | **Southern Wonders & Ritual** | Early morning hike through the thousands of *torii* gates at **Fushimi Inari Taisha Shrine**. Afternoon: Participate in a pre-booked **Tea Ceremony** experience (often found in the Gion or Higashiyama areas). |\\n| **4** | **Western Nature & Gardens** | Day trip to **Arashiyama**. Visit **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple** (UNESCO garden). Stroll through the **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove**. Consider taking the scenic Sagano Romantic Train (seasonal). |\\n| **5** | **Philosopher's Path & Serenity** | Walk the **Philosopher's Path** (best in spring/autumn). Visit **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion)** and its fine garden. Explore quieter northern Higashiyama temples like **Honen-in Temple** or **Kodai-ji Temple**. |\\n| **6** | **Deep Temple Dive & Tradition** | Visit the long hall of **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d** (1001 statues). Explore the sprawling **Daitoku-ji Temple** complex, known for its secluded Zen gardens and architecture. Optional: Try *Shojin Ryori* (Zen Buddhist vegetarian cuisine) near a temple like Tenry\u016b-ji or a specialized restaurant. |\\n| **7** | **Local Life & Departure** | Morning visit to a local market (e.g., Nishiki Market - be mindful of crowds/belongings). Final temple visit (perhaps **Tofuku-ji Temple** known for its autumn colors/gates). Departure preparations. |\"},\"events\":[{\"model_version\":null,\"content\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"Plan a 7-day cultural deep-dive travel experience in Kyoto focusing on temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature.\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"user\"},\"grounding_metadata\":null,\"partial\":null,\"turn_complete\":null,\"finish_reason\":null,\"error_code\":null,\"error_message\":null,\"interrupted\":null,\"custom_metadata\":null,\"usage_metadata\":null,\"live_session_resumption_update\":null,\"input_transcription\":null,\"output_transcription\":null,\"avg_logprobs\":null,\"logprobs_result\":null,\"cache_metadata\":null,\"citation_metadata\":null,\"invocation_id\":\"e-ff2ce17f-0f93-4bce-8ad9-806f42dc722d\",\"author\":\"user\",\"actions\":{\"skip_summarization\":null,\"state_delta\":{},\"artifact_delta\":{},\"transfer_to_agent\":null,\"escalate\":null,\"requested_auth_configs\":{},\"requested_tool_confirmations\":{},\"compaction\":null,\"end_of_agent\":null,\"agent_state\":null,\"rewind_before_invocation_id\":null},\"long_running_tool_ids\":null,\"branch\":null,\"id\":\"8ff8e2a7-f2ae-4038-b405-4abd9d1f17b5\",\"timestamp\":1763640927.823143},{\"model_version\":\"gemini-2.5-flash-lite-preview-09-2025\",\"content\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"Based on your request for a 7-day cultural deep-dive in Kyoto focusing on temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature, here is a comprehensive plan covering the best time to visit, key sights, cultural tips, transportation, and safety.\\n\\n***\\n\\n## Kyoto Cultural Deep-Dive: 7-Day Itinerary Planner\\n\\n### 1. Best Time to Visit and Weather Patterns\\n\\nThe best times to visit Kyoto for pleasant weather and beautiful scenery are **Spring (March to May)** and **Autumn (October to November)**.\\n\\n*   **Spring (March - May):** Ideal for viewing the famous cherry blossoms, typically from late March to early April. Weather is generally warm and comfortable, though rain is possible, especially towards late May.\\n*   **Autumn (October - November):** Considered one of the best times with lovely fall foliage peaking in mid- to late November. Weather is cool and comfortable, often sunny with dry air.\\n*   **Summer (June - August):** Hot and humid, with a rainy season from mid-June to late July.\\n*   **Winter (December - February):** Cold, with occasional snow, offering a more serene atmosphere.\\n\\n**Recommendation:** Aim for **October or November** for the best combination of comfortable sightseeing weather and stunning natural beauty to complement your temple visits. Be aware that spring and autumn are peak seasons, meaning larger crowds and higher prices.\\n\\n### 2. Top Attractions & Must-See Locations (Cultural Focus)\\n\\nKyoto has over 2,000 religious structures, including 17 UNESCO World Heritage Sites. For a cultural deep-dive, prioritize these:\\n\\n| Theme | Location | Key Feature/Cultural Significance |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Iconic Temples** | **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)** | A UNESCO World Heritage Site, famous for its top two floors covered in gold leaf, reflected in a pond. |\\n| **Zen & Nature** | **Ryoan-ji Temple** | Home to a world-famous Zen rock garden. |\\n| **Pilgrimage & Views** | **Kiyomizu-Dera Temple** | A revered World Heritage site known for its massive wooden veranda built without nails, offering views over Kyoto. |\\n| **Nature & Shinto** | **Fushimi Inari Taisha Shrine** | Famous for the thousands of vibrant red *torii* gates lining the hiking trails up the mountain. |\\n| **Gardens & Bamboo** | **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple** | A leading Zen temple in Arashiyama, known for its ancient and celebrated landscape garden (UNESCO site). |\\n| **Tradition & History** | **Gion District** | Kyoto\u2019s traditional entertainment district, famous for its preserved *machiya* houses and the chance to spot *Maiko* (apprentice geisha). |\\n| **Nature/Gardens** | **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove** | A serene area near Tenry\u016b-ji, perfect for a nature walk. |\\n\\n### 3. Local Culture, Customs, and Etiquette Tips\\n\\nKyoto highly values tradition and respect. Observing local etiquette will greatly enhance your experience.\\n\\n*   **Temples and Shrines:**\\n    *   **Respectful Attire:** Dress conservatively, covering shoulders, chest, and knees. Avoid very bright or flashy colors.\\n    *   **Footwear:** Be prepared to remove your shoes before entering temple buildings or tea rooms (*tatami* areas). Slip-on shoes are convenient.\\n    *   **Conduct:** Maintain quiet voices; temples are for prayer and worship.\\n    *   **Photography:** Observe signs carefully; photography is often prohibited inside buildings or with flash. Do not photograph worshippers without permission.\\n    *   **Purification Ritual (*Temizuya*):** If you use the water fountain, use the ladle to cleanse your left hand, then your right, then rinse your mouth (pouring water into your cupped left hand\u2014never touch the ladle to your lips), and finally rinse the ladle handle.\\n    *   **Bowing:** A slight bow when entering grounds is a sign of respect.\\n*   **General Etiquette:**\\n    *   **Tipping:** Tipping is **not customary** and is generally not appreciated. A polite \\\"Okini\\\" (thank you in Kansai dialect) is sufficient.\\n    *   **Noise:** Keep noise levels down, especially in sacred areas and on public transport.\\n    *   **Eating/Drinking:** Abstain from eating or drinking in sacred areas like temples and shrines.\\n    *   **Queuing:** Always line up in an organized queue when waiting for buses, trains, or shops.\\n    *   **Maiko/Geiko:** Do not bother, crowd, or touch *Maiko* (apprentice geisha), especially in the Gion area. They are busy and may not have time for photos.\\n*   **Tea Ceremony:** Be mindful of specific tea ceremony etiquette, such as handling the bowl and consuming the tea/sweets as instructed by your host. [This will be covered during the booking.]\\n\\n### 4. Transportation Options\\n\\nKyoto's public transport is extensive but can be somewhat complex as attractions are often not near subway stations.\\n\\n*   **Buses (Recommended for Tourists):** The **bus network is dense** and is generally considered the **best way to reach most major tourist attractions** like Kinkaku-ji and Kiyomizu-dera.\\n    *   **Payment:** Pay a fixed fare (cash only, exact change or 1000-yen notes accepted). You enter at the back and exit at the front. Consider a **Kyoto Bus Pass** for unlimited rides if you plan many stops.\\n*   **Subway & Trains:** Kyoto has two subway lines (Karasuma N-S, Tozai E-W) that are good for traveling north-south or east-west across the city center. Trains are useful for day trips (e.g., to Arashiyama, Nara).\\n*   **IC Cards:** Use an IC Card (like ICOCA, Suica, PASMO) for easy tap-and-go payment on virtually all trains and buses.\\n*   **Walking/Cycling:** The city center is relatively flat and excellent for walking or renting a bicycle for local exploration.\\n*   **Taxis:** Plentiful but more expensive; use them when public transport stops (around 10:30 PM) or for convenience. Most accept cash only.\\n\\n### 5. Safety Considerations and Travel Requirements\\n\\nKyoto is known as one of the safest cities in the world.\\n\\n*   **General Safety:** Crime rates are low, and lost items are often returned. Exercise common sense: be alert in crowded markets (like Nishiki Market) and avoid poorly lit paths at night, especially as a solo traveler.\\n*   **Traffic:** Be cautious of **bicycles on sidewalks**; if you hear a bell, move aside.\\n*   **Emergency Numbers:** Dial **110 for Police** and **119 for Ambulance/Fire**.\\n*   **Natural Disasters:** Be aware of the risk of earthquakes or typhoons. Prepare by having paper maps/contact info, extra medication, and knowing the location of the nearest Emergency Evacuation Site.\\n*   **Travel Requirements:** Check current visa/entry requirements for Japan well in advance. Tap water is clean.\\n\\n***\\n\\n## Sugge